emium. It has decent potential to make straights and high pairs.\n\n**Position & Action:** My opponent raised to 200 (double the big blind of 100), which is a standard opening raise. This doesn't reveal much about their hand strength.\n\n**Possible Actions:**\n\n1. **Fold (-1):** Too conservative. J-Q offsuit has reasonable equity in heads-up play (roughly 50-60% against random hands, 40-45% against typical raising ranges). Folding would be giving up too easily.\n\n2. **Call (0):** This would cost me 100 more chips (since I'm in the big blind and already have 100 in). This is a reasonable option that allows me to see the flop cheaply and evaluate further. Keeps the pot small when we're out of position.\n\n3. **Raise (400+):** A 3-bet to 400+ would show strength and put pressure on the opponent. However, J-Q offsuit isn't strong enough to want to build a huge pot pre-flop, especially out of position. If my opponent 4-bets, I'd likely have to fold, wasting chips.\n\n4. **All-in (-2):** Extremely aggressive and unnecessary with a medium-strength hand this early in the match. Would risk my entire stack on a marginal holding.\n\n**Stack Consideration:** I'm slightly ahead in the match (+400 cumulative), and we're only on hand 3 of 20. There's no need to take unnecessary risks.\n\nReason: \n\nJ-Q offsuit is playable in heads-up but not strong enough to justify aggressive pre-flop action like a large 3-bet or all-in. Calling allows me to see the flop cheaply, control pot size, and make better decisions with more information. If I hit top pair, a straight draw, or two pair, I can continue. If I miss completely, I can fold to aggression without investing too much. This is a disciplined, position-aware play that preserves my chip lead while giving my hand a chance to improve.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
